#
# Example-conformance cells for the finance slice's exact computations: a conforming
# simple-interest and holding-period-return pair, and one counter-example for each of a
# malformed dimension, a malformed frame and a malformed computation.
#
# Each cell unions the finance module with the one named file and validates it against the
# generated shapes that the finance module owns (crates/slicetest). Shapes owned by the
# observations, places and math slices are not loaded for this slice, so every fixture
# types locally the cross-slice individuals the finance-owned shapes check (the dimension
# as a math:Dimension, the period frames as gmeow:ReferenceFrame). The counter-examples are
# copies of the conforming fixture that differ by exactly one defect, and each pins the
# one law it breaks by IRI. A logic:Constraint projects to the shape named
# "<constraint IRI with its first letter upper-cased>ProceduralConstraintShape"
# (crates/logic-compile projections/shapes.rs, procedural_shape_iri), which for these
# urn: constraint IRIs begins "Urn:". The monetary amount's own dimension and currency-frame
# obligations are owned and controlled by the observations slice.

@prefix gmeow: <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/> .
@prefix ex:    <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/examples/finance/tests/> .

ex:ecExactComputations a gmeow:ExampleConformance ;
    gmeow:exampleFile "tests/conformance-fixtures/exact-computations.ttl" ;
    gmeow:expectedOutcome gmeow:conforms ;
    gmeow:conformanceRationale "A gmeow:SimpleInterestCalculation and a gmeow:HoldingPeriodReturnCalculation, with a gmeow:FinancialPeriodRate and a gmeow:FinancialPeriodCount that share the calculation's period frame, and two gmeow:MonetaryAmount values in one currency, validate with no finding. The fixture types math:dimensionless as a math:Dimension and the contract period as a gmeow:ReferenceFrame itself, because a conformance cell validates only the finance module plus this one file, and the math and places modules that declare those individuals are not loaded; without the local typing the finance-owned class checks on the rate and count would report an untyped dimension and frame that are well formed in the full corpus." .

ex:ecRateNotDimensionless a gmeow:ExampleConformance ;
    gmeow:exampleFile "tests/counter-examples/rate-not-dimensionless.ttl" ;
    gmeow:expectedOutcome gmeow:violates ;
    gmeow:expectedViolationCode "shacl.HasValueConstraintComponent" ;
    gmeow:expectedSourceShape gmeow:FinancialPeriodRate-shape ;
    gmeow:expectedSoleFinding true ;
    gmeow:conformanceRationale "The counter-example is the conforming fixture with one change: the gmeow:FinancialPeriodRate gives math:hasDimension as math:lengthDimension, a math:Dimension that is not math:dimensionless, so a rate would carry a physical dimension. The derived gmeow:FinancialPeriodRate-shape requires the value math:dimensionless on math:hasDimension (sh:hasValue), so validation MUST emit shacl.HasValueConstraintComponent from that shape and no other finding. The other value checks on the same path hold: the value is typed math:Dimension and there is exactly one." .

ex:ecRateFrameDiffersFromBasis a gmeow:ExampleConformance ;
    gmeow:exampleFile "tests/counter-examples/rate-frame-differs-from-basis.ttl" ;
    gmeow:expectedOutcome gmeow:violates ;
    gmeow:expectedViolationCode "shacl.SPARQLConstraintComponent" ;
    gmeow:expectedSourceShape <Urn:gmeow:finance:constraint:simple-interestRate-basisProceduralConstraintShape> ;
    gmeow:expectedSoleFinding true ;
    gmeow:conformanceRationale "The counter-example is the conforming fixture with one change: the gmeow:FinancialPeriodRate is stated on a second period frame while the gmeow:SimpleInterestCalculation names the original frame as its gmeow:calculationPeriodBasis. The constraint urn:gmeow:finance:constraint:simple-interestRate-basis requires the rate's gmeow:hasReferenceFrame to equal the calculation's period basis, so validation MUST emit shacl.SPARQLConstraintComponent from the shape that constraint projects to, pinned by IRI, and no other finding. The second frame is a complete scalar temporal frame, so the rate's own frame constraint holds, and the count still matches the basis, so the count's basis constraint holds." .

ex:ecReturnCurrencyMismatch a gmeow:ExampleConformance ;
    gmeow:exampleFile "tests/counter-examples/return-currency-mismatch.ttl" ;
    gmeow:expectedOutcome gmeow:violates ;
    gmeow:expectedViolationCode "shacl.SPARQLConstraintComponent" ;
    gmeow:expectedSourceShape <Urn:gmeow:finance:constraint:return-currency-matchProceduralConstraintShape> ;
    gmeow:expectedSoleFinding true ;
    gmeow:conformanceRationale "The counter-example is the conforming fixture with one change: the closing gmeow:MonetaryAmount of the gmeow:HoldingPeriodReturnCalculation is in the euro frame while the opening amount is in the dollar frame, so the return would divide a difference of unlike currencies. The constraint urn:gmeow:finance:constraint:return-currency-match requires the closing amount to share the opening amount's currency frame, so validation MUST emit shacl.SPARQLConstraintComponent from the shape that constraint projects to, pinned by IRI, and no other finding. The simple-interest calculation uses only the dollar principal, so it is unaffected." .
